# Icon Font Architecture

Which icon set is in use is a project decision — see `PROJECT.md`, and the
matching `project-*-icons` rule for slugs and codepoints. Everything here holds
regardless of the set.

**One curated set, one source of truth.** A single JS module lists the available
slugs and labels; every surface reads from it. Never let two surfaces maintain
independent icon lists.

## The four surfaces

| Surface | Renders as | Used when |
|---|---|---|
| Button block picker | Icon font glyph via CSS `::before` | Editing a `core/button` |
| PHP programmatic buttons | Icon font glyph, via the button helper | Buttons output from PHP |
| Sass mixins | Icon font glyph via CSS `::before` | Styling markup you don't control |
| Icon Block plugin | Inline SVG | Editors inserting a standalone icon |

Buttons use the **icon font**, not JS-injected SVG. Injecting `<svg data-lucide>`
markup and calling a library's `createIcons()` does not work reliably against the
iframed editor canvas — the script never runs there. CSS glyphs do.

## Enqueue rule

Icon CSS must render inside block **content** in both the editor iframe and the
frontend, so it enqueues on `enqueue_block_assets`. Using
`enqueue_block_editor_assets` loads it only in the editor chrome and triggers
`"...-style-css was added to the iframe incorrectly"`. See
`.cursor/rules/sf-build-pipeline.mdc`.

## Keep icon-font CSS out of the build

The `@font-face` + `content` rules live as a **static file in `assets/css/`**,
hand-maintained, outside webpack and Sass. Three pipeline behaviours forced this
and will recur with any icon package:

1. Packages declaring `"sideEffects": false` have their CSS import tree-shaken
   out of production builds.
2. Upstream icon CSS often embeds a multi-megabyte SVG font fallback in
   `@font-face`, which ends up inside `build/index.css`.
3. cssnano silently empties `content: "\eXXX"` escape values during
   minification.

The static file should contain only a minimal `woff2`/`woff` `@font-face` and
`content` rules for the icons actually curated — not the upstream full set.

## Subset the font, and generate it

Icon packages ship every glyph they have — Lucide's woff2 is 274KB for roughly
two thousand glyphs. A curated set uses a fraction of that, so ship a **subset**
built from the curated list, not the upstream file. For a typical curated set of
a few dozen icons that is 274KB against ~15KB, an order of magnitude that dwarfs
any saving from trimming the icon list itself.

Subset in the **same run that writes the codepoints**, from the same package
version. The failure this prevents is quiet and expensive: if the font is a
hand-copy and someone upgrades the icon package, the codepoint map moves while
the font does not, and every icon renders as some other icon with no error
anywhere. Generating both together makes the two incapable of disagreeing.

`subset-font` (harfbuzz via WASM) does this with no system dependencies, so it
works the same locally and in CI.

Two things to confirm before subsetting: nothing references the font family
outside the curated system, and no hardcoded codepoint escapes exist elsewhere.
Either would silently render tofu once the glyph is gone.

## Literal glyphs, not escape sequences

Where codepoints *do* go through Sass — the `$sk-icon-codepoints` map in
`src/sass/icon-font/_variables.scss` — each entry stores a **literal Unicode
character**, not a `"\eXXX"` escape string. This is load-bearing, for reason 3
above: literal characters survive minification, escapes get emptied.

Do not "clean up" that map into escapes. To regenerate or verify it, write a
short script that emits the file via `chr( int( hex, 16 ) )` — copy-pasting
Private Use Area characters through chat or an editor silently drops them. Then
verify the compiled output's raw bytes rather than trusting how the characters
render:

```bash
npm run build:assets
rg -o 'content:"."' build/public.css | xxd | head
```

## Class naming

Prefix every icon class with the project prefix. A previous generic `has-icon`
class collided with third-party plugin CSS.

```
sk-has-icon                     on the button wrapper when an icon is set
sk-icon-left / sk-icon-right    position
sk-icon-{slug}                  the specific glyph
```

## Sass mixin API

For plugin or WooCommerce markup where no class can be injected, target the
plugin's own selector:

```scss
// icon + label treatment: inline-flex, gap, correct pseudo-element
.add_to_cart_button { @include sk-icon-button( 'shopping-cart' ); }
.next-button        { @include sk-icon-button( 'arrow-right', right ); }

// bare pseudo-element, no layout changes
.some-selector    { @include sk-icon-before( 'search' ); }
.another-selector { @include sk-icon-after( 'chevron-down' ); }
```

These only add `font-family` and `content` — they rely on the `@font-face` being
loaded globally by the icon class, and do not re-declare it. The mixins are
forwarded from `src/sass/_tools.scss`; `@use` that, do not `@import`
`icon-font/mixins`.

## Stroke-based icons in the Icon Block plugin

The Icon Block plugin (`outermost/icon-block`) exposes an `iconBlock.icons` JS
filter for registering custom icons. Any icon set that is **stroke**-based
(`stroke="currentColor"`, `fill="none"`) must set `hasNoIconFill: true` on every
entry, or the plugin's colour control fights the icon's `fill="none"` and renders
incorrectly. Fill-based sets don't need it.

Strip `width`, `height`, and `class` from the root `<svg>` only — the plugin
controls sizing. Leave child shapes untouched.

## WordPress core's Icon block

Core ships `core/icon` backed by `WP_Icons_Registry`, closed to third parties in
current versions. WP 7.1 adds public `wp_register_icon_collection()` /
`wp_register_icon()`. Once core is new enough, register the same curated set as a
collection so the icons also appear in the native picker.

